{
 "what_you_observe": [
  "Tuesday, February 2. Seven pm.",
  "Phil said in the lobby: I say nothing to Rita. I say nothing to Larry. I nod once, past them, toward the door. That is the whole conversation.",
  "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
  "Larry said in the lobby: I say to whoever's still at the table: 'I'm taking the Rialto. You guys cover the ballroom tonight.' I don't wait for an answer. I'm already at the door with the bag over my shoulder.",
  "Larry records Alma on his last charged take. The reel stays with him.",
  "You are now at Rialto.",
  "There is time for one evening visit. The ballroom lights are on and the station line is open."
 ],
 "your_own_memories": [
  {
   "what_you_did": "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
   "what_you_said": "Ballroom's yours. I'm on the Rialto. One mic, one cable, a chair if her back's bad. I'll handle the rest.",
   "your_intention": "Be in the Rialto booth before the last reel loads onto the regional line. Sit beside Alma, not across. Let her talk. Record it. Send the file to Pittsburgh tonight with no title, no slug, no broadcast package. One voice, one daughter, one machine about to stop."
  }
 ],
 "who_you_are_with": "your present location",
 "physical_choices": {
  "transmit_interview": "Take the regional line at the station and send any usable interview with Alma to her daughter.",
  "go_dancing": "Spend the evening at the ballroom, open to dancing with a colleague.",
  "go_to_party_for_work": "Cover the party with your notebook, without dancing.",
  "take_evening_off": "Choose a quiet evening on your own."
 }
}

{
  "action": "transmit_interview",
  "detail": "I'm already at the Rialto. The booth is quiet. My patch is on the desk, the one I cut after Alma finished talking — the take where she said the name of the film that broke the first projector and the name of the one that didn't. The station line is open. I pick up the handset. I dial the Pittsburgh number the way it's written on the back of my notebook, not the way it's in the station directory. I route the patch through. I press send. I hold the handset to my ear and I listen for the confirmation tone, the one that means the file is moving, not sitting in a queue. It hums. The line holds. I set the handset down. I do not write a slug. I do not attach a memo. I do not call anyone to tell them I did it. I stand in the booth for a moment with the projector room dark behind me, the smell of hot dust and baked celluloid still in the air, and I breathe once, twice, and I walk out the side door into the February cold. The ballroom lights are still on across the street. They can stay on. I have nothing to cover tonight. I have a coat and a street and a night that is mine to walk through, and that is enough. I do not dance. I do not take notes. I do not explain to Phil, to Larry, to anyone, why the reel that matters is the one that just left the building. I walk. The station line is closed from my side now. The daughter in Pittsburgh will get a voice with no title on it, and that is the whole point, and that is the whole evening.",
  "intention": "One file, one voice, one daughter. Sent. Not packaged. Not slugged. Not a story. A mother talking to her kid across a machine that has run 42 winters and will not run a 43rd. The line is open now or it is not. I used it. That is the act. Everything else — the ballroom, the party, the dance, the notebook — is other people's evening. I am walking home through February and I am not going back to the booth to check the patch a second time. It's sent. It's moving. I let it move.",
  "words": "None. I do not say it to anyone. I do not need to say it to anyone. The hum in the handset was enough."
}
